description:
Location: src/backend.rs:116-123 (trait method), src/filenode.rs:232 (handler call site), src/memory.rs:668 (reference impl), src/lib.rs:446 (mock impl).

Trait signature:

    fn find_sibling_by_name(
        &self,
        caller: &Self::CallerCtx,
        account_id: &jmap_types::Id,
        parent_id: Option<&jmap_types::Id>,
        name: &str,
        case_insensitive: bool,                  // <- bool flag
    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<jmap_types::Id>, Self::Error>> + Send;

Call site in handler (filenode.rs:232):

    .find_sibling_by_name(
        caller,
        &account_id,
        parent_id_for_collision.as_ref(),
        &node_name,
        compare_case_insensitively,
    )

The supplement explicitly flags this pattern:

> bool parameters that are really two-variant enums in disguise.
> fn process(input: &str, verbose: bool) — the caller cannot tell at the
> call site what true means. A Verbosity enum is self-documenting.

A two-variant enum makes the call site self-documenting and pre-positions the API for the canonical fix to JMAP-510h.33 (case-folding algorithm is unspecified):

    /// Case-folding mode for sibling-name comparison.
    #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
    pub enum CaseFolding {
        /// Byte-for-byte comparison.
        Sensitive,
        /// Unicode Default Case Folding per Unicode Standard Annex §3.13
        /// over NFC-normalized input.
        Insensitive,
    }

Then:

    fn find_sibling_by_name(..., folding: CaseFolding) -> ...;

    // call site:
    .find_sibling_by_name(
        ...,
        if compare_case_insensitively { CaseFolding::Insensitive } else { CaseFolding::Sensitive },
    )

What goes wrong as-is — three things:

1. **Reader cost at every call site.** 'compare_case_insensitively' the local variable spells out the meaning at the inner call site, but a fresh reader of the trait method does not see the variable name — they see 'true' or 'false' if a downstream caller hardcodes it. The two boolean positions read as bare booleans in the rendered docs at https://docs.rs.

2. **API growth pressure.** The whole reason JMAP-510h.33 exists is that 'case_insensitive: bool' encodes the wrong shape. Real case-folding has more than two states: ASCII, Unicode simple, Unicode full, locale-dependent. A two-variant enum is the minimum step that lets the trait grow new variants (CaseFolding::AsciiOnly, CaseFolding::UnicodeFull) without a SemVer break (assuming #[non_exhaustive]). A bool freezes the API at 'two states, semantics undefined'.

3. **Trait-shape coupling with the spec.** draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-13 §3.2.3 specifies a bool ('compareCaseInsensitively') on the wire. The trait should not mirror the wire shape 1:1 when a better local shape is available. The wire bool can be converted at the handler boundary (the if-else above) — the trait does not need to be wire-isomorphic.

Canonical comparison: jmap-mail-server has no analogous case-folding bool. The closest equivalent (Mailbox lookup by name) is not part of the typed trait surface. So the bool flag is a filenode-specific shape choice, and the only one in the trait family that takes a boolean control flag.

Combined with JMAP-510h.33, the natural fix lands both findings: enum CaseFolding with documented semantics on each variant. Filed at idiomatic-rust,type-design P2 since the type-design improvement is independent of the algorithm-specification work in .33.
